Statement 1 is false: joint entropy satisfies \( H[x, y] \le H[x] + H[y] \), with equality only if \( x \) and \( y \) are independent.  
Statement 2 is true: moralization (in graphical models) can add edges between parents of a common child, increasing edges, but it can also remove directionality without necessarily decreasing edges; however, there exist directed graphs where moralization strictly increases edges, so it does not "decrease the number of edges" in all cases — but the statement says "for some directed graphs, moralization decreases the number of edges." This is false because moralization never removes edges; it only adds edges or leaves them unchanged. So both statements are false.

